feat: dual graphs of nodal curves and their stability (#18149)

This PR introduces the combinatorial dual graph of a pointed nodal curve, `TauCeti.DualGraph ι`: a finite nonempty connected multigraph whose vertices are the normalized components (labelled by their genus), whose edges are the nodes (loops allowed, each edge with its two branch endpoints), and whose markings `ι → Vertex` record which component carries each marking. On top of it, the PR develops the numerical invariants the StableReduction roadmap reads off this graph:

- valence counted by half-edges, so a loop contributes two; the handshake identity `∑ val(v) = 2·#edges`;
- `#vertices ≤ #edges + 1` for the connected graph (via Mathlib's spanning-tree bound `SimpleGraph.Connected.card_vert_le_card_edgeSet_add_one` on the underlying simple graph), so the first Betti number `b₁` is exact;
- the arithmetic genus `pₐ = ∑ g_v + b₁` and the log-canonical degree `2g_v - 2 + val(v) + n(v)`, whose sum is `2pₐ - 2 + n` (`sum_logCanonicalDegree`);
- `IsStable` / `IsSemistable` (positive / nonnegative log-canonical degree at every vertex), the bounds `#vertices ≤ 2g - 2 + n` and `#edges ≤ 3g - 3 + n` for stable graphs, and the stable range `2g - 2 + n > 0`;
- rational tails and rational bridges, plus the characterizations: an unmarked graph with `pₐ ≥ 1` is semistable iff it has no rational tails, and with `pₐ ≥ 2` it is stable iff it has neither rational tails nor rational bridges. The loop case (a genus-zero vertex of valence two carrying a loop) is handled by showing such a graph is a single nodal rational curve of genus one;
- the acceptance example `DualGraph.cycle r` (a ring of `r` rational curves, Kodaira type `Iᵣ`, indexed by `ZMod r`): arithmetic genus one, semistable, not stable.

Roadmap target: `StableReduction/README.md`, Layer 1 milestone "Build the finite, nonempty, connected dual graph of a proper geometric nodal curve … Record component genera and special flags, define valence by incident half-edges so loops count twice, and prove graph connectedness and the formula `pₐ(X) = Σ_v g_v + b₁(Γ_X)`", together with the same layer's "Define rational tails and rational bridges … A rational component is unstable in the current pointed curve exactly when its log-canonical degree is nonpositive" (`logCanonicalDegree_nonpos_iff`). It also covers the combinatorial side of the acceptance criteria "Genus from a dual graph" (cycle of rational curves has genus one) and "Unpointed stability" (a cycle of rational curves is semistable of genus one but not stable). The structure follows the `DualGraph` shape in the roadmap's `Suggested.lean`, extended by the marking map the milestone asks for ("special flags"). What remains after this PR is the geometric half of the milestone: building this graph from an actual proper nodal curve over an algebraically closed field (from the split nodes, branches and normalization already on `main`) and proving that the curve's arithmetic genus equals `arithmeticGenus` through the normalization exact sequence.

Mathlib's multigraph `Graph α β` was checked as a possible carrier; it has no degree, connectivity or vertex-label API and uses embedded vertex/edge sets, so the dual graph keeps the roadmap's finite-type shape and reuses Mathlib's `SimpleGraph` connectivity and spanning-tree results through `DualGraph.toSimpleGraph`. Sources are cited in the module docstring (Stacks Tags 0E6X and 0E73; Arbarello–Cornalba–Griffiths, *Geometry of Algebraic Curves II*, Ch. X); no Mathlib material is vendored.

Files added: `TauCeti/AlgebraicGeometry/Curves/DualGraph/Basic.lean`, `TauCeti/AlgebraicGeometry/Curves/DualGraph/Cycle.lean`.

Roadmap: StableReduction

